<h1>Trace a request</h1>
<p>Use a W3C trace ID to follow one message across gateway and broker, then distinguish the permission decision from the execution result.</p>
</header>
<nav class="guide-jumps" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#waterfall">Read a run</a><a href="#records">Find evidence</a><a href="#collection">Enable collection</a><a href="#privacy">Visibility and retention</a>
</nav>
<section id="waterfall">
<h2>Each process has its own exporter.</h2>
<figure class="trace-map">
<div><strong>Gateway</strong>
<ol>
<li>Receive message / session</li>
<li>Model request</li>
<li>Tool call → <code>shell.script</code>
<ul>
<li><code>shell.command</code> for each command</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Model continuation and chat reply</li>
</ol>
</div>
<div><strong>Broker · child of the tool request</strong>
<p><code>traceParent</code> crosses the Unix socket → authorization → provider invocation → host I/O</p>
<p><code>broker.decision</code> marks authorization; <code>broker.execution</code> records the result.</p>
</div>
<figcaption>Gateway → collector ← broker. Trace propagation correlates work; it grants no authority. This is an illustrative nesting map, not a measured latency chart.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Start from the chat-message trace, expand the model/tool span, then follow its broker child. Use the same trace ID in the log view to find decisions and results; an allow alone does not prove the remote write succeeded.</p>
</section>
<section id="records">
<h2>Ask the record the question it can answer.</h2>
<div class="guide-table" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Question comparison">
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col">Question</th>
<th scope="col">Record</th>
<th scope="col">Look for</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Who was allowed to act?</th>
<td data-label="Record">broker.decision</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Subject, principal, via, agent, action/resource and policy IDs.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Did the invocation succeed?</th>
<td data-label="Record">broker.execution + provider / HTTP spans</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Outcome, error classification and bounded result; distinguish upstream refusal from policy denial.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">What did the model run?</th>
<td data-label="Record">shell.script / shell.command</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Arguments, captured input/output, exit status and duration. Live byte streams are not a full packet capture.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Where did time and tokens go?</th>
<td data-label="Record">Model spans + transcript events</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Model requests/responses, tool calls and usage when supplied by the model API.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">How much model budget was charged?</th>
<td data-label="Record">meter · model call charged</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Agent, configured model, <code>meter.via</code> (agent/proxy/summary), charged input/output and <code>usage.source</code> (reported/estimated/partial). This log survives independently of sampled model spans when exported.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<th scope="row">Why did a provider hit its memory limit?</th>
<td data-label="Record">memory.store / memory.refused</td>
<td data-label="Look for">Per-store high-water accounting and the first refused growth. The old <code>provider.memory</code> record is gone; update saved queries.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>OpenRouter model spans may also carry reported <code>usage.cost</code> in USD, alongside its reported provider and generation ID. An absent cost stays absent; Dekopon does not compute a bill from token counts.</p>
<p>Token budgets count raw input plus output, including cached input. Boot restore uses agent/proxy <code>meter</code> logs, not model spans, and can under-count after collection loss. Summary calls are recorded separately and bypass budget admission and restore. A <code>meter.restore</code> warning means the gateway kept its live meters without retrying. <a href="/inference/#budgets">Budget and restore limits</a>.</p>
<p>Broker probes run as the mapped broker owner and only list authorized capabilities; they emit no decision audit. Use a real configured chat request to check decision/execution correlation.</p>
</section>
<section id="collection">
<h2>Configure telemetry in each daemon.</h2>
<p>Run a collector that accepts both OTLP traces and logs. Merge this block into your existing gateway configuration, then into broker configuration with <code>serviceName: dekopon-brokerd</code>. Replace the endpoint with your collector.</p>
<pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Telemetry configuration excerpt"><code>telemetry:
  endpoint: http://127.0.0.1:4318
  transport: http
  serviceName: dekopon-gatewayd
  exportTimeoutMs: 5000</code>
</pre>
<p>OTLP/HTTP is the only supported transport; <code>transport: grpc</code> refuses configuration. The exporter appends <code>/v1/traces</code> and <code>/v1/logs</code> to the base endpoint. Merely setting an endpoint environment variable does not enable the daemon’s configured export.</p>
<ol>
<li>Supply any collector authentication through <code>OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS</code> in each daemon’s environment, or a Kubernetes Secret reference. Do not put a token in command arguments or a config URL.</li>
<li>Restart both daemons with their updated configuration. Send a normal chat message that uses one granted provider command.</li>
<li>In the collector, find both service names under one trace ID and inspect the corresponding decision and execution logs. Missing broker children suggest propagation or broker export failure; missing logs with visible spans suggest the logs receiver or filtering.</li>
</ol>
<p><a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#startup">Deployment checks</a> · <a href="{{ source }}/docs/observability.md#enable-otlp-export">Exact exporter configuration ↗</a>
</p>
</section>
<section id="privacy">
<h2>The telemetry store contains user data.</h2>
<p>Prompts, model responses and script payloads are visible to the operator and configured backend. Command-supplied headers and bodies are data too; broker-injected credential values and native credential-bearing wire requests are excluded, not arbitrary sensitive text a user typed.</p>
<p>Command and proposal payload attributes are truncated at 4096 bytes with full-length byte counts. Prompt transcript events use <code>full</code> and <code>delta</code> records split into ordered segments of at most 16 KiB; opaque model reasoning is represented by a byte-count marker.</p>
<p>Sampling, <code>RUST_LOG</code> filtering and collection failures can leave gaps. Both processes offer records to bounded, nonblocking stdout queues; overload or sink failure can drop lines, reported by <code>telemetry.console.dropped</code>. Optional export is not a durable local audit archive.</p>
<p><strong>Lose collection and you lose the evidence.</strong> Configure backend retention and access controls yourself. Neither trace collection nor a restart undoes a posted comment.</p>
<p><a href="{{ source }}/docs/observability.md">Read the pinned event and field reference ↗</a>
</p>
</section>
